" (https://www.ietf.org/archive/id/draft-cui-dawn-mdi-model-02.txt)\nciteturn56view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/plain; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn55view1\",\"pattern\":\"artamokhov\"}); Total lines: 1539\nL1447:    The locator is assembled from the SRV or SVCB target and port plus\nL1448:    the path or well-known parameter or, absent both, the protocol's\nL1449:    default location (for A2A, /.well-known/agent-card.json).  Whether\nL1450:    the advertised location serves description retrieval or interaction\nL1451:    follows from the protocol: for A2A it serves the agent card, from\nL1452:    which interaction URLs are taken, while for MCP it is the interaction\nL1453:    endpoint [I-D.jakab-dawn-agent-discovery-mdns].\nL1454:    The individual drafts [I-D.zhang-dawn-bgp-style-federated-discovery]\nL1455:    and [I-D.zhang-dawn-agent-discovery-framework] define a TLV binding\nL1456:    of MDI elements for exchange between gateways, including an Entity-\nL1457:    Identifier and a Capability-Summary.  In\nL1458:    [I-D.zhang-dawn-bgp-style-federated-discovery], the Entity-\nL1459:    Identifier, which together with the Origin-GW-ID keys a record, can\nL1460:    serve as the Name and corresponds to the Stable Identifier; the\nL1461:    Capability-Card-URI is an Endpoint for description retrieval; and the\nL1462:    Capability-Summary, Trust-Reference, and Provenance TLVs correspond\nL1463:    to the information of the same names beyond the minimum\nL1464:    (Section 8.3).  That draft makes the Capability-Summary mandatory,\nL1465:    while Table 1 places coarse capability labels beyond the minimum.\nL1466: Acknowledgements\nL1467: \nL1468:    The author thanks Chenguang Du for his valuable contributions to the\nL1469:    design and text of this draft.\nL1470: \nL1471:    This work builds on the DAWN terminology, requirements, problem\nL1472:    statement, and gap-analysis documents.\nL1473: \nL1474:    Thanks to Aleksandr Artamokhov and Siyu Deng for their comments on\nL1475:    protocol versions, description changes, and Entity Type, and to\nL1476:    Riccardo Di Nuzzo for checking the procedure against draft-dinuzzo-\nL1477:    best-protocol.\nL1478: \nL1479: Authors' Addresses\nL1480: \nL1481: Cui & Yao                  Expires 6 May 2027                  [Page 30]\nL1482: \nL1483: \nL1484: Internet-Draft    Minimum Public Discovery Information     November 2026\nL1485: \nL1486: \nL1487:    Yong Cui\nL1488:    Tsinghua University\nL1489:    Beijing\nL1490:    100084\nL1491:    China\nL1492:    Email: cuiyong@tsinghua.edu.cn\nL1493:    URI:   http://www.cuiyong.net/\nL1494: \nL1495: \nL1496:    Kehan Yao\nL1497:    China Mobile\nL1498:    China\nL1499:    Email: yaokehan@chinamobile.com\nL1500: \nL1501: \nL1502: \nL1503: \nL1504: \nL1505: \nL1506: \nL1507: \nL1508: \nL1509: \nL1510: \nL1511: \nL1512: \nL1513: \nL1514: \nL1515: \nL1516: \nL1517: \nL1518: \nL1519: \nL1520: \nL1521: \nL1522: \nL1523: \nL1524: \nL1525: \nL1526: \nL1527: \nL1528: \nL1529: \nL1530: \nL1531: \nL1532: \nL1533: \nL1534: \nL1535: \nL1536: \nL1537: \nL1538: Cui & Yao                  Expires 6 May 2027                  [Page 31]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.ietf.org/archive/id/draft-cui-dawn-mdi-model-02.txt)\nciteturn56view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/plain; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn55view1\",\"pattern\":\"Acknowledgements\"}); Total lines: 1539\nL97: Cui & Yao                  Expires 6 May 2027                   [Page 2]\nL98: \nL99: \nL100: Internet-Draft    Minimum Public Discovery Information     November 2026\nL101: \nL102:    8.  MDI Information Model . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  14\nL103:      8.1.  Structure . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  15\nL104:      8.2.  Elements  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  16\nL105:      8.3.  Information Beyond the Minimum  . . . . . . . . . . . . .  18\nL106:      8.4.  Services  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  19\nL107:      8.5.  Minimal Constraints . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  20\nL108:    9.  Binding Expectations  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  21\nL109:    10. Security Considerations . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  22\nL110:    11. Privacy Considerations  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  23\nL111:    12. IANA Considerations . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  24\nL112:    13. Open Issues . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  24\nL113:    14. References  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  25\nL114:      14.1.  Normative References . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  25\nL115:      14.2.  Informative References . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  25\nL116:    Appendix A.  Illustrative JSON Example  . . . . . . . . . . . . .  27\nL117:    Appendix B.  Mapping Notes  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  28\nL118:    Acknowledgements  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  30\nL119:    Authors' Addresses  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  30\nL120: 1.  Introduction\nL121:    Clients often need to discover information about AI resources, such\nL122:    as AI agents, before interacting with them [DAWN-CHARTER], and often\nL123:    have no pre-configured relationship with them.  Before any\nL124:    interaction can begin, a client needs a small amount of information\nL125:    about the AI resource: what it is, what it does, how to reach it, and\nL126:    what assurances, if any, accompany those claims.\nL127:    [I-D.farrel-dawn-terminology] defines Minimum Discoverable\nL128:    Information (MDI) as the minimum amount of information an entity\nL129:    needs to provide to become discoverable, and does not define its\nL130:    content; [I-D.king-dawn-requirements] requires each discovery\nL131:    mechanism to define the MDI, including at least the entity's type,\nL132:    the services it offers, its reachability information, and the\nL133:    communication protocols and any security parameters needed to\nL134:    interact with it, and to indicate whether discovery information is\nL135:    static or dynamic; [I-D.akhavain-moussa-dawn-problem-statement] calls\nL136:    directly for a standardized structure carrying the minimum\nL137:    information a discovery response returns; and\nL138:    [I-D.moussa-dawn-gap-analysis] sketches a provisional baseline\nL139:    without normalizing it.  Mechanism-specific drafts define such fields\nL140:    for their carriers ([I-D.jakab-dawn-agent-discovery-mdns],\nL141:    [I-D.blwm-dawn-common-discovery-parameters]); none defines MDI\nL142:    content independently of a carrier.\nL143:    The DAWN charter [DAWN-CHARTER] places this information at the center\nL144:    of the working group's scope: discovery is limited to a client\nL145:    requesting and obtaining the minimum public discovery information for\nL146:    an AI resource (for example, its type, reachability information,\nL147: \nL148: \nL149: \nL150: Cui & Yao                  Expires 6 May 2027                   [Page 3]\nL151: \nL152: \nL153: Internet-Draft    Minimum Public Discovery Information     November 2026\nL154: \nL155:    communication protocol, and services) within a local network, within\nL156:    an organization, or between cooperating parties with trust\nL157:    relationships over the public Internet.  The charter assigns this\nL158:    information to two deliverables: the Discovery Architecture captures\nL159:    the requirements behind it, and the Protocol specifications, possibly\nL160:    one per deployment context, define it.  This document uses \"MDI\" as\nL161:    shorthand for the information the charter calls minimum public\nL162:    discovery information; where the two might differ, the charter's\nL163:    wording governs.\nL164:    Meanwhile, the gap is being filled independently and incompatibly by\nL165:    adjacent ecosystems: DNS-based bootstrap\nL166:    ([I-D.mozleywilliams-dnsop-dnsaid]), well-known JSON manifests, agent\nL167:    cards, and signed capability documents each carry a slightly\nL168:    different minimal field set with slightly different semantics, each\nL169:    bound to its own substrate.  A descriptor that is minimal and\nL170:    sufficient in one system is unreadable in another.\nL1466: Acknowledgements\nL1467: \nL1468:    The author thanks Chenguang Du for his valuable contributions to the\nL1469:    design and text of this draft.\nL1470: \nL1471:    This work builds on the DAWN terminology, requirements, problem\nL1472:    statement, and gap-analysis documents.\nL1473: \nL1474:    Thanks to Aleksandr Artamokhov and Siyu Deng for their comments on\nL1475:    protocol versions, description changes, and Entity Type, and to\nL1476:    Riccardo Di Nuzzo for checking the procedure against draft-dinuzzo-\nL1477:    best-protocol.\nL1478: \nL1479: Authors' Addresses\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.ietf.org/archive/id/draft-cui-dawn-mdi-model-02.txt)\nciteturn56view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/plain; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn55view1\",\"pattern\":\"version need not change\"}); Total lines: 1539\nL275:    Information, not fields:  Information that initial discovery needs\nL276:       can be carried in a discovery response, supplied by known context\nL277:       (such as the scope searched or the name the client started from),\nL278:       or fixed by a binding.  Necessary information therefore need not\nL279:       be repeated in every record.\nL280:    Thin core, rich references:  MDI is a header.  Rich or sensitive\nL281:       structures -- capability cards, proofs, certificates, policy\nL282:       documents -- are referenced, not embedded, and fetched only when\nL283:       needed.  A binding can carry small items beyond the minimum, such\nL284:       as coarse capability labels, directly as an Extension\nL285:       (Section 8.3).\nL350: Cui & Yao                  Expires 6 May 2027                   [Page 7]\nL351: \nL352: \nL353: Internet-Draft    Minimum Public Discovery Information     November 2026\nL354: \nL355:    \"that every discoverable entity provides\"; the charter defines\nL356:    discovery by what a client obtains.  The model of Section 8 describes\nL357:    MDI instances at both points: published MDI instances, which\nL358:    publishers provide, and the instances consumers obtain.  The\nL359:    correspondence need not be one to one.  One published MDI instance\nL360:    can yield several obtained instances, as when a Discovery Proxy\nL361:    relays an advertisement (Section 7), and an entity can have several\nL362:    published MDI instances, per protocol or per publisher; a binding\nL363:    states how they correspond.  A published and an obtained instance\nL364:    have the same elements and can differ in which values they carry and\nL365:    in the values themselves: a serving system can generate or transform\nL366:    values, as in the examples above; a binding can fix a value, such as\nL367:    an Endpoint purpose; and known context can supply one, such as a\nL368:    protocol named in the query.  Every other value comes from a\nL369:    publisher.\nL829:       carry the locator as one value, such as a URI, or assemble it from\nL836:       versions of the agent protocol; where it does not, the binding\nL837:       states how a client learns which version to use, for example from\nL838:       the description or through the protocol's own negotiation.  The\nL839:       purpose member says what the follow-up is: retrieving a\nL840:       description of the resource (for example, an agent card),\nL841:       interacting with the resource, or continuing discovery at another\nL842:       discovery service, such as a catalog.  When the purpose is not\nL843:       carried, the binding states how it follows from the protocol.  An\nL844:       Endpoint for description retrieval is how MDI points to a fuller\nL845:       capability description, such as a Capability Card; its protocols\nL846:       member names the agent protocol of the described resource, the\nL847:       description is fetched as the locator implies (for an https URI,\nL848:       with an HTTP GET), and the Endpoint can carry a digest of the\nL849:       description.  An entity can have Endpoints with different\nL850:       protocols and purposes.\nL851:    Freshness:  Version, validity window, and refresh hint for the\nL852:       instance.  A version need not change when the resource's\nL853:       description changes; the digest of a description Endpoint changes\nL854:       when the description does (Section 8.5).\nL855: \nL856:    The following elements are needed under the conditions that Section 7\nL857:    states.\nL858: \nL859:    Entity Type:  The kind of AI resource: for example an individual\nL860: Cui & Yao                  Expires 6 May 2027                  [Page 17]\nL861: \nL862: \nL863: Internet-Draft    Minimum Public Discovery Information     November 2026\nL864: \nL865:       named agent or a service such as a catalog that furthers\nL866:       discovery, the two kinds the charter names [DAWN-CHARTER], or\nL867:       another AI resource such as an AI inference service\nL868:       [I-D.farrel-dawn-terminology].  It lets a client triage before\nL869:       fetching anything, and is needed when the client has to tell kinds\nL870:       of AI resource apart to choose its next step.  It is usually\nL871:       implied by the carrier, such as a well-known name, or by the\nL872:       Endpoint purpose, and the binding states how.  The value space is\nL873:       left to DAWN, bindings, or profiles to define.\nL874:    Display Name:  A short human-readable name or description for a\nL875:       person choosing among results, with no machine semantics.  In DNS-\nL876:       SD the Name often serves; under unicast owner names, a binding\nL877:       carries one, as the title parameter of\nL878:       [I-D.blwm-dawn-common-discovery-parameters] does, or takes it from\nL879:       the description.  Language handling, if any, is a binding matter.\nL880:    Authentication Hint:  The authentication scheme(s) a discovering\nL881:       party would need for follow-up, optionally with a Reference to\nL882:       authentication metadata.  Never credentials, never authorization\nL883:       logic.\nL884:    Stable Identifier:  A name for the resource itself that stays the\nL885:       same across results, protocols, and time within its naming scope,\nL886:       so that a client can correlate results and recognize the resource\nL887:       later.  Where the naming scope is itself globally unique, such as\nL888:       a DNS domain or a URN namespace, the Stable Identifier is globally\nL889:       unique.  Like a Name, it is a name, not a verified identity, key,\nL890:       or proof.\nL891:    Extension:  An extension named by a binding, profile, or future\nL892:       registry, carrying type-specific or ecosystem-specific\nL893:       information, including the information beyond the minimum of\nL894:       Section 8.3.  An Extension does not alter core element semantics\nL895:       and does not reintroduce a non-goal of Section 3.\nL896: \nL897: 8.3.  Information Beyond the Minimum\nL898:    Table 1 finds that a client does not need the following information\nL899:    for its next step, so it is not part of MDI; REQ-PROP-1 of the\nL900:    individual requirements draft [I-D.king-dawn-requirements] likewise\nL901:    states that \"Properties beyond the MDI are optional\".  Existing\nL902:    formats and individual drafts carry it (Appendix B).  A binding that\nL903:    carries it does so as an Extension, with the following meanings:\nL904: \nL905:    Capability Summary:  Coarse, machine-readable capability tags for\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.ietf.org/archive/id/draft-cui-dawn-mdi-model-02.txt)\nciteturn56view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/plain; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn55view1\",\"pattern\":\"conditional\"}); Total lines: 1539\nL622:    The results are the same in all three contexts; what differs is where\nL623:    the client gets each item.  On a local network, each result carries\nL624:    the name, the endpoint location, and the protocol.  A query for a\nL625:    known name, as under a cooperating party's domain, supplies the name,\nL626:    and a query for one protocol supplies the protocol.  The binding\nL627:    supplies defaults, such as the protocol's default path, and usually\nL628:    the endpoint purpose, which follows from the protocol or from a well-\nL629:    known name such as an index label.  Freshness comes from the lifetime\nL630:    of the records a result is built from.\nL631:    The conditional items depend on the next step.  A display name\nL632:    matters when a person chooses among results.  An authentication hint\nL633:    matters when the client interacts directly and neither a description\nL634:    nor the agent protocol's own handshake supplies the scheme first.  A\nL635:    Stable Identifier matters when results are correlated, as when a\nL636:    client within an organization sees the same agent both through\nL637:    multicast DNS on its link and through a Discovery Proxy\nL638:    (Section 8.5).  Entity type matters when the client has to tell kinds\nL639:    of AI resource apart to choose its next step, and is usually implied\nL640:    by the carrier, such as a well-known name, or by the endpoint\nL641:    purpose.  Whether services are covered by Endpoint protocols and\nL642:    Entity Type is asked in Section 13.  Information beyond the minimum\nL643:    can help but is not needed for this initial step: the client chooses\nL644:    by protocol and type, learns the details from the description, and\nL645:    learns availability at the follow-up connection.\nL646:    Where the results are meant for an audience, as \"available to\nL647:    employees\" and \"for all its customers/members\" suggest, the\nL648:    restriction applies to the lookup, for example through split-view DNS\nL649:    or a VPN, and the result carries nothing that enforces it; where this\nL650:    requester context belongs is asked in Section 13.\nL651: \nL652: \nL653: \nL654: \nL655: \nL656: \nL657: \nL658: \nL659: \nL660: \nL661: Cui & Yao                  Expires 6 May 2027                  [Page 13]\nL662: \nL663: \nL664: Internet-Draft    Minimum Public Discovery Information     November 2026\nL665: \nL666:    Origin is where the contexts differ most.  Under a party's domain,\nL667:    DNSSEC-validated records and TLS-authenticated origins tie the\nL668:    information to the party.  A catalog or a Discovery Proxy that relays\nL669:    information can be authenticated only as itself, and on a local link\nL670:    multicast DNS does not authenticate advertisements, so the agent is\nL671:    authenticated only at the follow-up connection\nL672:    [I-D.jakab-dawn-agent-discovery-mdns].  How the charter's integrity\nL673:    requirement is met for such advertisements is asked in Section 13.\nL674:    Compared with REQ-PROP-1 of the individual requirements draft\nL675:    [I-D.king-dawn-requirements], reachability and protocol are needed in\nL676:    every context, type and security parameters (an authentication hint)\nL677:    under a condition, and services are a question for the working group.\nL678: \nL679: 8.  MDI Information Model\nL680: \nL681:    An MDI instance is the common header published or obtained for one\nL682:    entity.  Its elements follow the results of Section 7 (Table 1):\nL1171:    *  Is the procedure of Section 6 an acceptable basis for deciding\nL1172:       what belongs in the minimum public discovery information?  Are the\nL1173:       results of Section 7 right, including the conditions under which\nL1174:       an entity type, a display name, an authentication hint, or a\nL1175:       Stable Identifier is needed, and is the information that Table 1\nL1176:       places beyond the minimum correctly left out of MDI?  If so,\nL1177:       should this document keep naming that information for bindings\nL1178:       (Section 8.3), or leave it to bindings and profiles?\nL1179:    *  Should MDI be defined only by what a client obtains, or also at\nL1180:       publication?  This document proposes both points, with Status\nL1181:       defined for the instance a consumer obtains and a publisher's\nL1182:       obligations derived from it per binding (Section 5); an\nL1183:       alternative is to set Status separately for published MDI\nL1184:       instances.\nL1185:    *  Which items of the MVD should DAWN specify for each deployment\nL1186:       context?  Is requester context, such as the audience that\nL1187:       \"available to employees\" or \"for all its customers/members\"\nL1188:       implies, part of the MVD, and if so who supplies it, or is it\nL1189:       access control outside discovery?  Is information restricted to\nL1190:       such an audience public in the charter's sense?\nL1191:    *  How should the charter's examples \"type\" and \"services\" be read?\nL1192:       This document makes Entity Type conditional and usually implied by\nL1193:       the carrier or the Endpoint purpose, covers services by the\nL1194:       protocols of Endpoints and by Entity Type, and places coarse\nL1195:       labels (Capability Summary) beyond the minimum (Section 8.4); is\nL1196:       that right?\nL1197: Cui & Yao                  Expires 6 May 2027                  [Page 24]\nL1198: \nL1199: \nL1200: Internet-Draft    Minimum Public Discovery Information     November 2026\nL1201: \nL1202:    *  Where an agent's advertisement or registration is not\nL1203:       authenticated to its operator, as with multicast DNS, a Discovery\nL1204:       Proxy relaying it, or SRP, how is the charter's requirement for\nL1205:       integrity of the information exchanged met: by authenticating the\nL1206:       agent at the follow-up connection, by that together with\nL1207:       protection of the hop that delivers the information, such as a\nL1208:       Discovery Proxy's DNSSEC signature, or only by protecting the\nL1209:       advertisement to its origin, for example with authenticated DNS\nL1210:       Update ([RFC8766], Section 9.1), which places a requirement on\nL1211:       registration (Section 5)?  See Section 7.\nL1212: 14.  References\nL1213: \nL1214: 14.1.  Normative References\nL1215: \nL1216:    [I-D.farrel-dawn-terminology]\nL1217:               Farrel, A., Yao, K., Schott, R., and N. Williams,\nL1218:               \"Terminology for the Discovery of Agents With Names\nL1219:               (DAWN)\", Work in Progress, Internet-Draft, draft-farrel-\nL1220:               dawn-terminology-06, 28 October 2026,\nL1221:               <https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-farrel-dawn-\nL1222:               terminology-06>.\nL1223:    [RFC2119]  Bradner, S., \"Key words for use in RFCs to Indicate\nL1224:               Requirement Levels\", BCP 14, RFC 2119,\nL1225:               DOI 10.17487/RFC2119, March 1997,\nL1226:               <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2119>.\nL1227: \nL1228:    [RFC8174]  Leiba, B., \"Ambiguity of Uppercase vs Lowercase in RFC\nL1229:               2119 Key Words\", BCP 14, RFC 8174, DOI 10.17487/RFC8174,\nL1230:               May 2017, <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8174>.\nL1231: \nL1232: 14.2.  Informative References\nL1233:    [DAWN-CHARTER]\nL1234:               IETF, \"Discovery of Agents With Names (dawn) Working Group\nL1235:               Charter\", October 2026,\nL1236:               <https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/charter-ietf-dawn/>.\nL1237:    [I-D.akhavain-moussa-dawn-problem-statement]\nL1238:               Akhavain, A., Moussa, H., and D. King, \"Problem Statement\nL1239:               for the Discovery of Agents, Workloads, and Named Entities\nL1240:               (DAWN)\", Work in Progress, Internet-Draft, draft-akhavain-\nL1241:               moussa-dawn-problem-statement-05, 19 July 2026,\nL1242:               <https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-akhavain-\nL1243:               moussa-dawn-problem-statement-05>.\nL1244: Cui & Yao                  Expires 6 May 2027                  [Page 25]\nL1245: \nL1246: \nL1247: Internet-Draft    Minimum Public Discovery Information     November 2026\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.ietf.org/archive/id/draft-cui-dawn-mdi-model-02.txt)\nciteturn56view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/plain; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn55view1\",\"pattern\":\"learns\"}); Total lines: 1539\nL622:    The results are the same in all three contexts; what differs is where\nL623:    the client gets each item.  On a local network, each result carries\nL624:    the name, the endpoint location, and the protocol.  A query for a\nL625:    known name, as under a cooperating party's domain, supplies the name,\nL626:    and a query for one protocol supplies the protocol.  The binding\nL627:    supplies defaults, such as the protocol's default path, and usually\nL628:    the endpoint purpose, which follows from the protocol or from a well-\nL629:    known name such as an index label.  Freshness comes from the lifetime\nL630:    of the records a result is built from.\nL631:    The conditional items depend on the next step.  A display name\nL632:    matters when a person chooses among results.  An authentication hint\nL633:    matters when the client interacts directly and neither a description\nL634:    nor the agent protocol's own handshake supplies the scheme first.  A\nL635:    Stable Identifier matters when results are correlated, as when a\nL636:    client within an organization sees the same agent both through\nL637:    multicast DNS on its link and through a Discovery Proxy\nL638:    (Section 8.5).  Entity type matters when the client has to tell kinds\nL639:    of AI resource apart to choose its next step, and is usually implied\nL640:    by the carrier, such as a well-known name, or by the endpoint\nL641:    purpose.  Whether services are covered by Endpoint protocols and\nL642:    Entity Type is asked in Section 13.  Information beyond the minimum\nL643:    can help but is not needed for this initial step: the client chooses\nL644:    by protocol and type, learns the details from the description, and\nL645:    learns availability at the follow-up connection.\nL646:    Where the results are meant for an audience, as \"available to\nL647:    employees\" and \"for all its customers/members\" suggest, the\nL648:    restriction applies to the lookup, for example through split-view DNS\nL649:    or a VPN, and the result carries nothing that enforces it; where this\nL650:    requester context belongs is asked in Section 13.\nL651: \nL652: \nL653: \nL654: \nL655: \nL656: \nL657: \nL658: \nL659: \nL660: \nL661: Cui & Yao                  Expires 6 May 2027                  [Page 13]\nL662: \nL663: \nL664: Internet-Draft    Minimum Public Discovery Information     November 2026\nL665: \nL666:    Origin is where the contexts differ most.  Under a party's domain,\nL667:    DNSSEC-validated records and TLS-authenticated origins tie the\nL668:    information to the party.  A catalog or a Discovery Proxy that relays\nL669:    information can be authenticated only as itself, and on a local link\nL670:    multicast DNS does not authenticate advertisements, so the agent is\nL671:    authenticated only at the follow-up connection\nL672:    [I-D.jakab-dawn-agent-discovery-mdns].  How the charter's integrity\nL673:    requirement is met for such advertisements is asked in Section 13.\nL674:    Compared with REQ-PROP-1 of the individual requirements draft\nL675:    [I-D.king-dawn-requirements], reachability and protocol are needed in\nL676:    every context, type and security parameters (an authentication hint)\nL677:    under a condition, and services are a question for the working group.\nL678: \nL679: 8.  MDI Information Model\nL680: \nL681:    An MDI instance is the common header published or obtained for one\nL682:    entity.  Its elements follow the results of Section 7 (Table 1):\nL683:    *  Information needed in every context becomes the mandatory Name and\nL684:       Endpoint (location, agent protocol, and purpose) and the Freshness\nL685:       element.  Freshness is SHOULD because its meaning is obtainable\nL686:       from the carrier lifetime; a binding whose carrier provides no\nL687:       lifetime MUST carry it.\nL688:    *  Information needed only under a condition becomes an element with\nL689:       Status MAY: Entity Type, Display Name, Authentication Hint, and\nL690:       Stable Identifier.  A publisher cannot always know whether the\nL691:       condition holds for a given consumer; a binding or profile for\nL692:       deployments where it holds can make the element MUST; for example,\nL693:       a binding for exchange between gateways or catalogs can make the\nL694:       Stable Identifier MUST.\nL695:    *  Services, left to the working group, are covered by Endpoint\nL696:       protocols and Entity Type (Section 8.4).\nL697: \nL698:    *  Origin is a protection goal, met by the binding (Section 9), and\nL699:       is not an element.\nL700: \nL701:    *  Information beyond the minimum is not an element of MDI.  A\nL702:       binding can still carry it as an Extension, and Section 8.3 names\nL703:       the kinds that existing formats and individual drafts carry, so\nL704:       that bindings agree on their meaning.\nL705:    Mandatory elements keep the record very small; in constrained\nL706:    carriers, a binding can derive their values from the carrier or from\nL707:    known context (Section 9).  All Status values are provisional.\nL708: \nL709: \nL710: \nL711: \nL712: Cui & Yao                  Expires 6 May 2027                  [Page 14]\nL713: \nL714: \nL715: Internet-Draft    Minimum Public Discovery Information     November 2026\n"
